==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|fro}} ''[[aquest]]'', French ''[[acquêt]]'', from {{etyl|LL.}} ''[[acquestum]]'', {{term|acquisitum|acquisītum|lang=la}}, for Latin {{term|acquisitum|acquisītum|lang=la}}, past participle (used substantively) of {{term|acquirere||lang=la|to acquire}}. See “[[acquire]]”

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{rare}} [[acquisition]]; the thing gained. -- [[w:Francis Bacon|Francis Bacon]].
# {{legal}} property acquired by purchase, gift, or otherwise than by inheritance. --  [[w:John Bouvier|John Bouvier]].

====Usage notes====
Bouvier, 1856, [http://www.constitution.org/bouv/bouvier_a.htm] from which the Webster derives, uses the spelling ''[[acquets]]''.

